WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.600
We're all familiar with the legendary ideal of&nbsp;
outlaws like Robin Hood. Living on the outskirts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:17.360
of English society, robbing from the wealthy,&nbsp;
and giving to the poor. Based on such tales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:21.040
one might assume that being an outlaw&nbsp;
in the medieval era was synonymous with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:27.440
being a criminal -- but this is far from&nbsp;
the case. Being an outlaw was terrifying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.960
because you were literally declared to be&nbsp;
'outside' the protection of the 'law.' In short,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:39.200
if you were an outlaw and someone were to murder&nbsp;
you, that person wouldn't face legal consequences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:45.200
The same thing goes for any other crime you can&nbsp;
think of. Possessions, money, and any land you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:51.680
once owned also became forfeit the moment you were&nbsp;
declared an outlaw. It was almost like the purge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.680 --> 00:01:00.560
but it only applied to you. Being declared a&nbsp;
felon in most countries today is much different.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:05.520
Committing a serious offense in most modern&nbsp;
nations might result in a lengthy prison sentence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:11.840
but the system of laws still theoretically applies&nbsp;
to you. Just because you're on trial for robbery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.800
for example, it wouldn't be legally permissible&nbsp;
for someone to break into the courthouse and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:23.520
shoot you with an arrow... to put it bluntly but&nbsp;
in medieval Europe, if you were an outlaw you had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:28.480
no such right to safety. Examining the&nbsp;
text of the medieval common law of England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:36.000
a Wirt of Outlary made with respect to you Kaput&nbsp;
Lupinum. literally meaning 'let his be a wolf's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.680
head.' Legally speaking, the term essentially&nbsp;
equates you with a wolf, or any other wild beast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.880
in the eyes of the law. It's a roundabout way of&nbsp;
indicating anyone who felt keen to hunt you down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:52.400
as if you were an animal would face no punishment&nbsp;
for it. Indeed there was often some reward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:59.120
either social or economic, for doing so. But&nbsp;
outlaws weren't just hunted by random citizens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:03.440
As fugitives, outlaws were pursued by&nbsp;
county sheriffs and other forms of feudal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:09.920
law enforcement which were notoriously brutal.&nbsp;
Furthermore, it was actually illegal for anyone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:15.920
even family members, to give you aid: including&nbsp;
basic necessities like food and shelter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:23.920
Once a Wirt of Outlawry had been declared, life&nbsp;
was extremely rough. The crazy history of Outlawry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:29.720
extends all the way back to ancient Roman laws of&nbsp;
exile. The penalty was then referred to as 'homo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:36.240
sacer,' Latin for the accursed one, and meant --&nbsp;
as with medieval society -- the unlucky individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:41.280
was forced from civilization and deprived&nbsp;
of all civic rights. Exile also included&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.880
the related punishment of 'aqua at igni&nbsp;
interdictus:' literally translating as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:53.840
to 'forbid water and fire. This imposing addendum&nbsp;
meant what it implies: codifying the illegality of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:59.040
providing outlaws with basic necessities, pretty&nbsp;
similar to medieval laws around offering aid.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:05.120
Examining the history of outlawry in other&nbsp;
countries, in early Germanic law, the death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:11.040
penalty is notably absent altogether, with&nbsp;
being made an outlaw the most severe punishment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:15.760
Since the label was so dangerous,&nbsp;
this makes a grim amount of sense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:22.080
Being made an outlaw was, essentially, a fate&nbsp;
worse than death. Much later in the 16th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:27.200
the German concept of 'vogelfrei' took on a&nbsp;
similar meaning. The denotation of the term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:32.640
is similar to 'free as a bird,' which began as&nbsp;
a positive description implying independence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.520
With time however, being a vogelfrei meant&nbsp;
you had the same legal rights as a bird,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:42.720
or beast, due to criminal actions. And as with&nbsp;
other cultures, meant your days were numbered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:48.480
Exactly what constitutes a crime serious enough&nbsp;
to be made in outlaw depended on the culture and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:53.520
time period, with everything from robbery,&nbsp;
to murder, to not showing up for court when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:59.520
summoned being listed as examples. The legendary&nbsp;
outlaw of English folklore robin hood might have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:05.360
certainly faced such a punishment were he a real&nbsp;
historical figure in 13th or 14th century CE,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:09.200
making the character a bit more&nbsp;
hardcore than he's often remembered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:16.640
A notable historic outlaw is Norse warrior Eric&nbsp;
the Red, who is exiled in 982 CE for killing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:21.440
another member of his community. ot interested in&nbsp;
sticking around to be potentially killed, while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:26.800
an outlaw he sailed north and might have helped&nbsp;
discover Greenland. Pretty interesting stuff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:33.280
Going full circle and returning to English common&nbsp;
law, the outlaw punishment is thankfully no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:37.680
in effect in the modern day. Funnily enough&nbsp;
though, the law wasn't officially abolished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:44.000
in England until as late as 1938: although the&nbsp;
concept was already obsolete, and wasn't actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:49.280
being used. Still, there's something interesting&nbsp;
about the fact that outlaw punishment technically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:54.880
survived all the way up until the invention of&nbsp;
the car. Were the punishment of outlawry brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.880 --> 00:05:00.080
back today, it would be extremely terrifying&nbsp;
in our modern society, so we can all be glad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:06.240
we don't live in the medieval era. And that's&nbsp;
where a discussion of ancient outlaws reaches&nbsp;&nbsp;

